<h2>Routes</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th>Route</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code>GET /</code>, <code>/css/*</code>, <code>/js/*</code>, <code>/learn/*</code></td><td>Static files from <code>public/</code> and <code>dist/client/</code> (path traversal blocked)</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>GET /api/config</code></td><td>Is Jira configured? Never returns the token.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>POST /api/config</code></td><td>Save URL, email, token; verified against <code>/rest/api/3/myself</code> first</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>DELETE /api/config</code></td><td>Delete saved credentials</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>GET /api/bugs?key=</code></td><td>Epics view</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>GET /api/mine</code>, <code>GET /api/issue?key=</code></td><td>For You and detail</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>GET /api/jarvis/status</code>, <code>/events</code>, <code>/stream</code></td><td>Monitor status, history, live Server-Sent Events</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>POST /api/jarvis/prefs</code>, <code>/read</code>, <code>/clear</code>, <code>/sync</code></td><td>Settings, read state, clear history, check now</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>How it protects you even on localhost</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Binds to 127.0.0.1</strong>: other machines on your Wi-Fi cannot connect.</li>
  <li><strong>Host check</strong>: requests must say <code>Host: localhost:4545</code> or <code>127.0.0.1:4545</code>. This defeats "DNS rebinding", a trick where a malicious website points its own domain at 127.0.0.1.</li>
  <li><strong>Origin + custom header</strong> on every non-GET request, so another website open in your browser cannot change settings.</li>
  <li><strong>Credentials file</strong> in your home folder with permission 0600 (only your user can read it), never in the project folder, so it cannot be committed to Git.</li>
  <li><strong>System notifications</strong> are sent with <code>execFile</code> and an argument list, never through a shell, so text from Jira cannot become a command.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The local monitor (<code>src/server/jarvis.ts</code>)</h2>
<p>A simpler cousin of the cloud monitor, because it runs for one user with no strict limits:</p>
<ol>
  <li>Every interval (30 s to 15 min, default 60 s): one paginated search lists every bug assigned to you.</li>
  <li>The list is compared with the previous snapshot: new keys → <code>classifyNew</code>; changed <code>updated</code> → read changelog and comments → <code>classifyChanges</code>; keys that disappeared → look the issue up → <code>classifyAway</code>.</li>
  <li>Events get increasing ids, are appended to the history (max 500), and are written with the snapshot and <code>seen</code> ids to <code>jarvis.json</code> atomically (write a temporary file, then rename).</li>
  <li>Each connected browser tab receives the events over its open SSE connection; status updates are sent too.</li>
  <li>If no tab is connected and you enabled it, macOS (<code>osascript</code>) or Linux (<code>notify-send</code>) shows a system notification.</li>
  <li>Failures back off exponentially (honouring Jira's <code>Retry-After</code>); bad credentials wait 10 minutes.</li>
</ol>
<p>The classification functions are the shared ones in <code>src/core/detect.ts</code>, so both editions announce exactly the same things. See <a href="jarvis-engine.html">JARVIS: how changes are detected</a>.</p>

<h2>Server-Sent Events in 30 seconds</h2>
<p>The browser opens <code>new EventSource('api/jarvis/stream')</code>. The server answers with <code>Content-Type: text/event-stream</code> and never closes the response. Whenever it has news it writes:</p>
<pre><code>id: 42
event: jarvis
data: {"id":42,"type":"comment","key":"ABC-1",…}

</code></pre>
<p>The browser reconnects automatically if the connection drops and sends <code>Last-Event-ID</code>, so the server replays what was missed. A comment line (<code>: ping</code>) every 25 seconds keeps proxies from closing the idle connection.</p>

<h2>Configuration options</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th>Variable</th><th>Effect</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code>PORT</code></td><td>Port (default 4545)</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>JIRA_URL</code>, <code>JIRA_USERNAME</code>, <code>JIRA_API_TOKEN</code></td><td>Use these instead of the saved file (never written to disk)</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>SEVERITY_FIELDS</code>, <code>ISSUE_TYPES</code></td><td>Override severity field names and issue types</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>JBD_CONFIG_DIR</code></td><td>Where to keep config.json and jarvis.json (tests use a temporary folder)</td></tr>
</table>
